// The starting skill vocabulary. Phase 14.
//
// Read by SkillSeeder at startup, after the Skills migration. Every write it
// makes is conditional: an existing skill is never renamed, an existing Kind or
// Category is never overwritten, and an alias that collides with a skill name is
// skipped and logged. So this file can be edited and the app restarted.
//
// THE ONE RULE FOR ADDING AN ALIAS: it must be a SYNONYM, not a relative.
//
//   "Docker Containers" -> "Docker"        yes, same thing, different words
//   ".NET Core"         -> ".NET"          yes, Microsoft renamed it
//   "PostgreSQL"        -> "SQL"           NO. One is an instance of the other.
//   "Azure"             -> "Cloud"         NO. Broader term.
//   "React"             -> "JavaScript"    NO. Built with, not equal to.
//
// The failure mode of a wrong alias is invisible and expensive: the skill gap
// silently reports a match the CV has not earned, and nothing errors. When in
// doubt, make it its own skill — two rows that should be one is a nuisance, one
// row that should be two is a lie.
//
// Weighted to what Melbourne .NET/backend advertisements actually name, plus the
// frontend, cloud, data and DevOps terms that turn up beside them.
//
// Comments and trailing commas are allowed here for the same reason they are in
// appsettings*.json — the reader is configured for them. Don't "fix" them.
